A||||11||batboy||Latest computer upgrades||||6||
Z||000000||batboy||10-28-2000||08:45 PM||||I'm turning into a computer hardware geek I think. I began overhauling my home computer this last summer. Basically, I spaced out buying my components to a couple per month. I get paid twice a month, hence why I used this plan.<P>What did I keep from my old computer? A 17" Samsung monitor, mid-tower ATX case, Diamond MX-300 A3D sound card, Cambridge Soundworks 4 speakers and a powered subwoofer sound system, 128 meg of RAM memory, 13 gig Maxtor harddrive, 3.5" floppy drive, and SyQuest SparQ backup removeable drive.<P>I started out by replacing my Celeron 300A CPU chip (overclocked to 450 MHz) with a Celeron 566 chip that I promptly overclocked to 850 MHz. After I sold my old chip, I bought a new Abit SE6 motherboard and then sold my old Abit BH6 mobo.<P>Once I got the board running stable, I invested in a HP CD-writer Plus 8250i. Next I got a new, fast 40 gig Maxtor 7200 rpm ATA 100 EIDE harddrive. Suddenly, my old CD-ROM drive died, so I replaced it with the worlds fastest, a Kenwood True-X multi-beam CD-ROM drive.<P>Ok, I wanted the top of the line GeForce 2 GTS 64 meg video card, but could not justify $400, so I got a Hecules 3D Prophect II MX (a GeForce 2 MX 32 meg card). Awesome 3D graphics and it's one of the latest 4X AGP cards. Best card on the market for under $150 in my opinion.<P>Last, I've got another 128 meg. stick of RAM memory (PC133 CAS2) and a 3Com 56K modem with a built on processor (not a WinModem) ordered. The only thing left is to replace my joystick. I'm leaning towards the Microsoft Force Feedback one, it's really cool.<P>Well, that should satisfy all my computer gaming and net surfing needs for this winter. I'm very happy with how everthing turned out. I did a lot of research and shopping. Looks like it paid off. Happy computing!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000001||Terrybogin||10-28-2000||09:04 PM||||Do you think that you could help my computer? It is a 386sx 16mhz with an 180 meg hard drive. Why does it take win95 so long to load?||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSTETZ||10-29-2000||12:12 AM||||add a math coprocessor, and use msconfig to remove all the startup items, and that 386 will boot win95 faster than most pentiums. i have 13 computers living with me (im in school to be a networking geek, have all the computers tied to a win2k server) and the 386 is the toy, its ridiculous. i will have to post some pics. i put a JCW ABS hood scoop on it and painted flames. sad part is that the hood scoop is bolted over a hole with a fan in it, so it is cold air induction.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1012904&a=7460599&p=25516673"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||10-29-2000||12:30 AM||||I thought that I was one of the few with the in-home network. I had only three computers linked. But instead of educational purposes, I would invite a couple of friends over so we could play Duke Nukem Death Match. <BR>BTW- Terrybogin is old and slow like his computer- he would get hurt with anything faster  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Cooter (edited 10-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||10-29-2000||02:37 AM||||I had a 386 DX once, 20 blazing MHz of speed, 4 meg of RAM, 130 meg harddrive, both 3.5" and 5.25" floppy drives, and a 14" superVGA monitor that barely had 12" of viewing area. I paid $2,000 for that machine in 1991. Not counting my scanner and color deskjet printer, I have less money wrapped up in this new system.<P>Cheever, I've had my setup running at 950 MHz a few times. When I get my new PC133 CAS 2 RAM, I'm going to try to break the 1 GHz barrier. I forgot to mention cooling. I'm using a Golden Orb cooler on my CPU, a front case fan, and an old 486 CPU fan mounted on the heatsink of my video card. Of course the power supply has it's own fan.||||reg||1
